course: dictionary
lesson: 2956
title: Weather words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Drizzly describes weather where light, fine rain keeps falling steadily. The raindrops are so small and gentle that you may hardly feel them, but they do not stop.
explain: A drizzly day is not a storm. There is no thunder and no wind, just a quiet, damp rain that goes on for hours.
explain: Writers use drizzly to set a mood. Look at the clue in «"Dreich" is Scottish for dull, grey and drizzly weather.» Grey skies and drizzle together make a gloomy feeling, so a drizzly day in a story is often a sad or lonely day.
fact: Drizzly is an adjective, so it can change to drizzlier and drizzliest.
fact: Drizzly means weather with light, fine rain falling steadily.
fact: «Dreich» (Scotland) means dull, grey and drizzly.
fact: Drizzly weather is very common in the UK and Ireland.
fact: A drizzly day is different from a rainstorm: the rain is light, but it keeps going.
exercise: «"Dreich" is Scottish for dull, grey and drizzly weather.» What does drizzly describe? | A strong wind blowing over the sea | Thick snow piling up on the roads | Hot sunshine on a beach | *Weather with light, fine rain falling steadily
exercise: Kirsty's mum says the dreich Scottish winter is grey and drizzly. Which kind of day is drizzly? | A night with a full moon and no clouds | A day of bright sunshine and blue sky | A day with a huge thunderstorm | *A day of light rain that goes on and on
exercise: Why might a writer describe a town as drizzly? | To show a bright, happy festival | To show a hot summer holiday | *To set a grey, gloomy mood | To show a busy market full of food
test: Which word means nearly the same as drizzly weather in Scotland? | *dreich | servo | bday | fixin' to
test: Which sentence describes a drizzly day? | A gale tore the roof off the barn | The lake froze solid overnight | *Light rain fell steadily all afternoon | The sun blazed and the sand was hot
exam: Which description fits a drizzly day best? | Bright sun with a cloudless sky | *Fine rain that falls steadily without stopping | Thick snow that piles up in the street | A storm with thunder and lightning
